<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Holy Alliance</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Holy Alliance was an agreement signed in September 1815 by the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, proposed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a>, by which they pledged to govern according to Christian principles. Under Metternich's influence it came to stand for the conservative powers' cooperation in suppressing liberal and national revolutions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/holy-alliance.jpg" alt="Holy Alliance" width="320" height="325" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cukrakalnis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Holy_Alliance_(1840).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Holy Alliance, also called the Grand Alliance, linked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchies</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. It was created after the final defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> at the behest of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I of Russia</a> and signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 26 September 1815. Alexander wanted to instil the divine right of kings and Christian values in European political life, under the influence of his spiritual adviser Baroness Barbara von Krüdener. The text was written by Alexander and edited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannis-kapodistrias">Ioannis Kapodistrias</a> and Alexandru Sturdza.</p><p>Under the treaty European rulers would govern as &quot;branches&quot; of the Christian community and offer one another mutual service. The first draft appealed to mysticism through a proposed unified Christian empire, which the other monarchies found disconcerting, and a more pragmatic version was adopted. About three months after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/final-act-of-the-congress-of-vienna">Final Act of the Congress of Vienna</a>, the monarchs of Catholic Austria, Protestant Prussia and Orthodox Russia promised to act on the basis of &quot;justice, love, and peace&quot;. The French diplomat Dominique-Georges-Frédéric Dufour de Pradt called the document &quot;an apocalypse of diplomacy&quot;.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The agreement was at first secret and was mistrusted by liberals. The United Kingdom rejected it, though George IV consented as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, as did the Papal States and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castlereagh">Castlereagh</a>, the British Foreign Secretary, called it &quot;a piece of sublime mysticism and nonsense&quot;. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> nevertheless took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concert-of-europe">Concert of Europe</a>.</p><p>In practice the Austrian chancellor, Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klemens-von-metternich">Klemens von Metternich</a>, made it a bastion against democracy and nationalism. It also coordinated the suppression of Polish efforts to restore an independent state, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodomeria">Lodomeria</a>, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in Congress Poland and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Posen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-prussia">West Prussia</a>. At the Congress of Aix-la-Chapelle in 1818 the monarchs demanded stern measures against university demagogues, carried out in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlsbad-decrees">Carlsbad Decrees</a> of 1819. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opava">Troppau</a> in 1820 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Laibach</a> in 1821 Metternich sought to align his allies against the Carbonari revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-the-two-sicilies">Ferdinand I of the Two Sicilies</a>, and in 1822 the Congress of Verona advised against the Greek Revolution and resolved on the French invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The alliance is conventionally taken to have become defunct with Alexander's death in 1825, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> went its own way after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/july-revolution">July Revolution</a> of 1830. The three eastern powers agreed the Münchengrätz Convention in September 1833 and met again to suppress the revolutions of 1848. The Austro-Russian alliance broke up in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> declared itself neutral and occupied the Danubian Principalities in 1854. Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a> reunited the three powers as the League of the Three Emperors after German unification in 1871, but it faltered in the 1880s over the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Holy Alliance” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The revolutionaries failed to court popular support and fell to Austrian troops of the <b>Holy Alliance</b>, after which Ferdinand abolished the constitution and persecuted known revolutionaries.</blockquote><a href="/movement/unification-of-italy">Unification of Italy · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
